    "q-137920": {
      "dom_id": "step-q137920",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $a+\\frac{b}{c}?$",
      "worked_solution": "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -12 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}45 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 20\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}60 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}45 & 30 & 60 \\\\ 30 & 20 & 40\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}45 & 30 & 60 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{3})}{1}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
